    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d259">

                <pb facs="0750" n="707" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v27/pg707"/>

                <head>259. Memorandum of Conversation<anchor n="1" xml:id="d259-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" type="conversation-or-meeting-date"
                            when="1974-08-26">August 26, 1974</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>PARTICIPANTS</label>
                    <item>President Ford</item>
                    <item>Dr. Henry A. Kissinger, Secretary of State and Assistant to the President
                        for National Security Affairs</item>
                    <item>Lt. General Brent Scowcroft, Deputy Assistant to the President for
                        National Security Affairs</item>
                </list>
                <p>Kissinger: The Kurds are conducting a guerrilla war inside Iraq. If Iraq weren’t
                    tied down, they could put more into Syria. [Described the program.]<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d259-f2"/></p>
                <pb facs="0751" n="708" xml:id="pg708"/>
                <p>The danger is the Kurds will collapse. We have exhausted our [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">less than 1 line not declassified</hi>] equipment. [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">2 lines not declassified</hi>]</p>
                <p>President: What does this do to our argument about supplying out of active
                    units?</p>
                <p>Kissinger: The numbers aren’t that significant.</p>
                <p>President: Let’s go ahead.</p>
                <p>Kissinger: The Iranians have asked about sending troops in. The CIA guy is
                    opposed. We said it is their decision<anchor n="3" xml:id="d259-f3"/>—I favor it
                    but I hate to be on record because of all the leaks.</p>
                <p>President: I read that the Iraqi army is unhappy. Are the Kurds reliable?</p>
                <p>Kissinger: Yes. It is their existence at stake.</p>
                <p>President: [<hi rendition="#italic">1 line not declassified</hi>]</p>
                <p>Kissinger: [<hi rendition="#italic">1 line not declassified</hi>]</p>
                <p>[Omitted here is discussion unrelated to Iraq.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d259-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d259-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Ford Library, National Security Adviser, Memoranda of Conversations, Box
                    5. Secret; Nodis. The meeting was held in the Oval Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f2" n="2" xml:id="d259-f2-content">
                <p>Brackets in the original.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f3" n="3" xml:id="d259-f3-content">
                <p>Telegram 24914 from Tehran, August 24, informed Kissinger of Barzani’s request
                    for Iranian troops with anti-tank and anti-aircraft weapons to join his forces.
                    (Ford Library, National Security Adviser, Backchannel Messages, Box 4,
                    Mideast/Africa, Incoming 8/74) Kissinger replied in backchannel message WH42555,
                    August 24: “This must be basically an Iranian decision. We will understand
                    whatever they decide to do.” (Ibid., Outgoing 8/74)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
